ht": 0.6, "fundamentals": {"margin": -5.04, "lean": -4.5, "environment": 4.2, "incumbency": -4.0, "money": -0.74}, "rating_margin": -4.86, "sigma_race": 5.5, "sigma_total": 5.92, "baseline_margin": -2.99, "analyst_adjustment": 0.99, "key_factors": ["Recent polls show a dead heat, with several October surveys giving Hinojosa a small lead", "Abbott's incumbency, massive cash advantage ($67M on hand), and Texas's Republican lean", "Prediction markets still price Abbott around 80%, well ahead of polling-implied odds", "Expert ratings cluster at Lean R despite the tight polling average"], "rationale": "The polling average has tightened to roughly a two-point Abbott lead, and several high-quality October polls show Hinojosa even or ahead, justifying a shift from the fundamentals-based Lean R picture. But Abbott's incumbency, overwhelming fundraising edge, and markets pricing him near 80% argue against going further toward a tossup.", "watch": "Early voting turnout patterns and whether Democratic outside spending materializes could tip a race this close either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Greg Abbott (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Texas governor is far closer than usual this cycle. Incumbent Greg Abbott faces Democrat Gina Hinojosa, and recent polls show the race essentially tied, with several October surveys giving Hinojosa a narrow edge and the overall average around a two-point Abbott lead. Headlines describe Republicans privately worried and Democrats beginning to spend in the state.\n\nStill, Abbott holds major structural advantages: he has raised $25 million with $67 million on hand, dwarfs Hinojosa's $6 million, and benefits from incumbency in a state that has leaned Republican by mid-single digits. Expert raters mostly call the race Lean R, and prediction markets still give Abbott roughly an 80% chance. The forecast lands at a two-point Abbott edge with elevated uncertainty, a genuine Lean R rather than a comfortable one.", "history": [{"da